in the six weeks since the bank of  England last met the set interest rates  there have been two prime ministers two  chancellors and a crisis of confidence  in the UK economy the bank's response  the biggest single interest rate  increase is way back in 1989 a 0.75  percentage Point increase taking rates  to three percent their highest since the  2008 financial crisis and it says rates  will rise further but perhaps not as  high as the current Peak the market  expects of close to five percent it's  acted because inflation is running at  more than 10 it could Peak at 11 again  the highest it's way back in the 1980s  and a product of high energy prices  exacerbated by the war in Ukraine the  banks worry is that this imported  inflation could become embedded in the  UK economy as wages rise to keep up so  by increasing rates the bank is trying  to squeeze demand and bring inflation  down and what does it mean for mortgage  holders well today's increase alone will  have added 700 pounds a year to  repayments on a one hundred thousand  pound track a mortgage and fixed rates  have soared look at this the current  average rates for two and five year  fixed mortgage mortgages they're both  more than six percent a huge increase in  cost imposed in just the last few weeks  and the bank is clear responsibility for  a chunk of that extra cost lies with Liz  truss and quasi-quarting this chart  shows the yield on 10-year UK guilds  there are proxy a measure for government  borrowing the Blue Area shows the impact  of what the bank says are international  factors shared across the world the red  is what it calls the UK premium that's  an additional cost of more than two  percent since August imposed on  homeowners businesses and government  made worse by the mini budget in  September that's what happens next  that's the bleakest bit of the bank's  Outlook it's forecasting a recession  lasting up to two years this shows its  projections for GDP the size of the  economy and it's getting smaller the  pink line the higher line here is the  path of a recession if rates remained at  three percent we know they'll go higher  the darker line is if they increase in  line with Market expectations the  reality is it'll be somewhere in here in  between but it still means a recession  lasting deep into 2024 and only slow  recovery after that with unemployment  peaking at around six and a half percent  and at no point does the bank's forecast  say the economy will return to  pre-covered sizes the UK it seems will  pay a high price for the pandemic for  war in Europe and political turbulence  at home
 

